 pirate headquarters south of Shanghai.","cp":"Wokou pirates","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiajing_wokou_raids","c":2},{"y":1557,"t":"loss","x":"1557: Macau is given to the Portuguese.","cp":"portuguese-empire","cpn":"Portuguese Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty","c":2},{"y":1559,"t":"occupation_end","x":"1559: the Yangtze delta is cleared.","cp":"Wokou pirates","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiajing_wokou_raids","c":2},{"y":1562,"t":"occupation_start","x":"1562: Xinghua (Putian) falls to the pirates.","m":12,"cp":"Wokou pirates","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiajing_wokou_raids","c":3},{"y":1563,"t":"loss","x":"1563: the Chinese Shan States go to Bayinnaung.","cp":"first-toungoo-empire","cpn":"Toungoo Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taungoo_Empire","c":2},{"y":1563,"t":"occupation_end","x":"1563: the last pirate base in Fujian is destroyed 'by May 1563'.","m":5,"cp":"Wokou pirates","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiajing_wokou_raids","c":3},{"y":1567,"t":"vassal_end","x":"1567: the Tibetan court relation lapses.","cp":"tibet","cpn":"Tibet","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming%E2%80%93Tibet_relations","c":2},{"y":1600,"t":"gain","x":"1600: the Yunnan frontier is back, bridging.","cp":"first-toungoo-empire","cpn":"Toungoo Empire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taungoo_Empire","c":1},{"y":1618,"t":"contest_start","x":"1618: Nurhaci opens the war in Liaodong.","cp":"later-jin-dynasty","cpn":"Later Jin dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_conquest_of_the_Ming","c":3},{"y":1618,"t":"loss","x":"1618: Fushun falls to the Jin.","cp":"later-jin-dynasty","cpn":"Later Jin dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_conquest_of_the_Ming","c":3},{"y":1618,"t":"vassal_end","x":"1618: the Jianzhou Jurchens renounce the Ming.","cp":"jianzhou-jurchens","cpn":"Jianzhou Jurchens","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty","c":3},{"y":1619,"t":"loss","x":"1619: Tieling falls to the Jin.","cp":"later-jin-dynasty","cpn":"Later Jin dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurhaci","c":3},{"y":1621,"t":"contest_end","x":"1621: the Liaodong fighting ends in a Jin victory.","m":5,"cp":"later-jin-dynasty","cpn":"Later Jin dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurhaci","c":3},{"y":1621,"t":"loss","x":"1621: Liaoyang and Shenyang fall.","m":5,"cp":"later-jin-dynasty","cpn":"Later Jin dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurhaci","c":3},{"y":1622,"t":"loss","x":"1622: the rest of Liaodong goes, bridging.","cp":"later-jin-dynasty","cpn":"Later Jin dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurhaci","c":1},{"y":1637,"t":"vassal_end","x":"1637: Joseon breaks with the Ming.","cp":"joseon","cpn":"Joseon","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseon","c":3},{"y":1642,"t":"loss","x":"1642: Jinzhou surrenders.","m":4,"cp":"later-jin-dynasty","cpn":"Later Jin dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_conquest_of_the_Ming","c":3},{"y":1643,"t":"loss","x":"1643: Zhang Xianzhong takes Wuchang and proclaims himself 'Xi Wang' (King of the West).","cp":"xi-dynasty","cpn":"Xi dynasty","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhang_Xianzhong","c":2},{"y":1643,"t":"loss","x":"1643: Li Zicheng holds Xiangyang.","cp":"li-zicheng","cpn":"Shun dynasty (Li Zicheng)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Zicheng","c":2},{"y":1643,"t":"loss","x":"1643: Li Zicheng takes Xi'an and Shaanxi.","cp":"li-zicheng","cpn":"Shun dynasty (Li Zicheng)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Zicheng","c":2},{"y":1644,"t":"vassal_end","x":"1644: Ryukyu's Ming tribute passes to the Southern Ming.","cp":"ryukyu-kingdom","cpn":"Ryukyu Kingdom","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyu_Kingdom","c":2},{"y":1644,"t":"loss","x":"1644: Li Zicheng takes Shanxi.","m":3,"cp":"li-zicheng","cpn":"Shun dynasty (Li Zicheng)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_conquest_of_the_Ming","c":2},{"y":1644,"t":"loss","x":"1644: Beijing falls to Li Zicheng.","m":4,"cp":"li-zicheng","cpn":"Shun dynasty (Li Zicheng)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_conquest_of_the_Ming","c":3},{"y":1644,"t":"dissolve","x":"1644: the Ming end at Beijing.","m":4,"cp":"li-zicheng","cpn":"Shun dynasty (Li Zicheng)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty","c":3}],"wars":[{"name":"Ming conquest of Yunnan","from":1381,"to":1383,"outcome":"Ming victory: the Yuan prince of Liang died on 6 January 1382 and Dengchuan, the last Dali stronghold, fell in February 1383.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_conquest_of_Yunnan"},{"name":"Ming-Northern Yuan wars of the Hongwu era","from":1370,"to":1388,"outcome":"Ming victories at Karakorum (May 1370), Yingchang (June 1370) and Buir Lake (1388); the Yuan court survives as the Northern Yuan on the steppe.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hongwu_Emperor"},{"name":"Ming campaign against Naghachu","from":1387,"to":1388,"outcome":"Naghachu surrendered and the Ming took Manchuria (1387) and the Liaodong peninsula (1388).","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty"},{"name":"Ming-Mong Mao War","from":1386,"to":1398,"outcome":"Two Möng Mao rebellions (1386-1389, 1397-1398) were put down; no border moved.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_conquest_of_Yunnan"},{"name":"Yongle Emperor's campaigns against the Mongols","from":1410,"to":1424,"outcome":"Inconclusive: 'Weakening of both sides; Status quo ante bellum'; the Emperor died on 12 August 1424 on the return from the fifth campaign.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yongle_Emperor%27s_campaigns_against_the_Mongols"},{"name":"Fourth Chinese domination of Vietnam / Lam Sơn uprising","from":1406,"to":1427,"outcome":"The Ming took Đại Ngu in 1406-07 and held it as the province of Jiaozhi (1407-1427); the Lam Sơn rebels, rising on 7 February 1418, won the decisive battle on 7 November 1426 and the Ming withdrew in December 1427.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourth_Chinese_domination_of_Vietnam"},{"name":"Luchuan-Pingmian campaigns","from":1438,"to":1449,"outcome":"Ming victory on the infobox, four campaigns (December 1438, 27 February 1441, March 1443, March 1449); Si Renfa executed in January 1446; the last campaign 'ended inconclusively'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luchuan%E2%80%93Pingmian_campaigns"},{"name":"Tumu Crisis","from":1449,"to":1450,"outcome":"Mongol victory at Tumu (1 September 1449 on the Tumu page, 8 September on the Ming page); Esen reached Beijing on 27 October and withdrew; 'There were no territorial changes'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tumu_Crisis"},{"name":"Ming-Mongol border wars to the Altan Khan peace","from":1550,"to":1571,"outcome":"The 1550 invasion reached the suburbs of Beijing; walls were built at Xuan-Da (1544-49) and Jizhou-Changping (1569-71); 'Altan Khan eventually made peace with China when it opened border cities for trade in 1571'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Great_Wall_of_China"},{"name":"Jiajing wokou raids","from":1539,"to":1567,"outcome":"'Ming victory': the pirate bases at Shuangyu (1548), the Yangtze delta (1559) and Xinghua (1563) were destroyed and the sea ban was lifted in 1567.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiajing_wokou_raids"},{"name":"Imjin War (Ming relief of Joseon)","from":1592,"to":1598,"outcome":"'Ming China intervened on the side of the Koreans, sending a large force in 1593'; no Ming border moved.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseon"},{"name":"Ming-Later Jin war","from":1618,"to":1644,"outcome":"The Jin took Fushun (1618), Tieling (1619), Shenyang and Liaoyang (1621) and Jinzhou (8 April 1642); the Ming held the Ningyuan corridor until Wu Sangui opened Shanhai Pass in 1644.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_conquest_of_the_Ming"},{"name":"Late Ming peasant rebellions","from":1629,"to":1644,"outcome":"Li Zicheng took Xiangyang (1643), Xi'an (late 1643), Taiyuan (March 1644) and Beijing (April 1644); Zhang Xianzhong took Wuchang (1643) and Sichuan (1644).","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Zicheng"}],"certainty":[{"from":1368,"to":1470,"level":"soft","note":"The Ming's early frontiers are approximate: the steppe edge, Tibet, the south-western tusi and Annam (1407-1427); the provincial borders inside are the Yuan ones."},{"from":1471,"to":1644,"level":"soft","note":"The northern frontier follows the Great Wall once it is built (the Yulin wall approved on 20 December 1472 and finished in 1474; the Xuan-Da walls completed in 1548; the Jizhou-Changping towers 1569-71) and is firmer there; Tibet, the…"}],"sources":[{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty","title":"Ming dynasty"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hongwu_Emperor","title":"Hongwu Emperor"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_conquest_of_Yunnan","title":"Ming conquest of Yunnan"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourth_Chinese_domination_of_Vietnam","title":"Fourth Chinese domination of Vietnam"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lam_S%C6%A1n_uprising","title":"Lam Sơn uprising"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurgan_Regional_Military_Commission","title":"Nurgan Regional Military Commission"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tumu_Crisis","title":"Tumu Crisis"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Great_Wall_of_China","title":"History of the Great Wall of China"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming%E2%80%93Tibet_relations","title":"Ming-Tibet relations"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hami","title":"Hami"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yongle_Emperor%27s_campaigns_against_the_Mongols","title":"Yongle Emperor's campaigns against the Mongols"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luchuan%E2%80%93Pingmian_campaigns","title":"Luchuan-Pingmian campaigns"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseon","title":"Joseon"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qing_conquest_of_the_Ming","title":"Qing conquest of the Ming"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurhaci","title":"Nurhaci"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jianzhou_Jurchens","title":"Jianzhou Jurchens"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Zicheng","title":"Li Zicheng"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhang_Xianzhong","title":"Zhang Xianzhong"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiajing_wokou_raids","title":"Jiajing wokou raids"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyu_Kingdom","title":"Ryukyu Kingdom"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taungoo_Empire","title":"First Toungoo Empire"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_emperors_of_the_Ming_dynasty","title":"List of emperors of the Ming dynasty"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordos_Mongols","title":"Ordos Mongols"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty_military_conquests","title":"Ming dynasty military conquests"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hetao","title":"Hetao"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Yunnan","title":"History of Yunnan"}]}